IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `safw-dates`
IMPORT `safw-core`

-- Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) -- THE SEAT OF THE EMPLOYEE: the employee or former employee (or outworker) bringing a claim
-- under the Act -- for unpaid money, severance on redundancy, an outworker's unpaid remuneration, a civil penalty for
-- unpaid entitlements, relief for unfair dismissal, or redress for dismissal for a prohibited reason. Every question is
-- asked as that person would ask it, from facts that person can state; SAET's, the Court's and the President's views
-- are asked as conditions ("SAET is satisfied that ..."). The rules are in safw-core.l4; this module restates none.
--
-- Based on content from the South Australian Legislation website (version of 22 August 2026, fetched 6 November
-- 2026). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `The employee's questions`

§§ `Am I covered by the Act (ss 4, 5, 6)`

@export Am I an "outworker" under the Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 5?
GIVEN `engaged for another's business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are engaged for the purposes of another person's trade or business, either yourself or through a company for which you personally do all or a substantial part of its work
      `processing or clerical work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the engagement is to work on, process, clean or pack articles or materials, or to carry out clerical work
      `at a home or non-business premises` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that work is done in or about a private residence, or other premises not conventionally regarded as a place of business
      `arranging outworkers' work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the engagement is to negotiate or arrange work by outworkers, or to distribute work to or collect it from outworkers
      `cleaning someone else's home` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are engaged by one person to clean the private residence of a third person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Am I an outworker` MEANS `safw is an outworker (s 5(1)-(3))` `engaged for another's business` `processing or clerical work` `at a home or non-business premises` `arranging outworkers' work` `cleaning someone else's home`

@export Does the Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) apply to my employment, so that I can bring claims under it (ss 4, 5(4), 6)?
GIVEN `my engagement` IS AN `Engagement` @desc How you are engaged: a contract recognised at common law as a contract of employment for remuneration; to drive a vehicle not registered in your name for a public passenger service; to personally clean premises; to work as an outworker; or some other engagement
      `a taxi` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you drive for a public passenger service: whether the vehicle is a taxi
      `an outworker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an outworker under s 5 (see the outworker question)
      `outworkers brought in` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are an outworker: whether a provision of an award or enterprise agreement relates to outworkers, or a regulation extends the Act to outworkers
      `a public employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a public sector employee under the Public Sector Act 2009, or otherwise employed for salary or wages in the service of the State of South Australia
      `employed by family` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer is your spouse, domestic partner or parent
      `excluded by the regulations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Fair Work Regulations exclude your employment from the Act
      `national system employer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer is a national system employer under the Commonwealth Fair Work Act 2009 (most private businesses in South Australia are; State and local government employers are generally not)
      `excluded subject matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter is an "excluded subject matter" under the Fair Work (Commonwealth Powers) Act 2009 (SA), which the State law still governs
GIVETH AN `Application answer`
`Does the Act apply to my employment` MEANS
    `safw does the Act apply to my employment (ss 4, 5(4), 6)` (`safw is a contract of employment (s 4(1))` `my engagement` `a taxi` `an outworker`) `a public employee` `an outworker` `outworkers brought in` `employed by family` `excluded by the regulations` `national system employer` `excluded subject matter`

§§ `Bringing an application to SAET (s 17(2))`

@export Can I bring my application to the South Australian Employment Tribunal as of right, or must I first show a genuine grievance (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 17(2))?
GIVEN `authorised elsewhere in the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another provision of the Act authorises your application (for example an unfair dismissal application under s 106, or a severance application under s 72B(5))
      `genuine grievance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether SAET is satisfied that your claim arises out of a genuine industrial grievance
      `no other process available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether SAET is satisfied that no other impartial grievance resolution process is, or has been, reasonably available to you
GIVETH AN `As of right answer`
`Can I bring my application to SAET` MEANS `safw can I bring the application to SAET (s 17(2))` `authorised elsewhere in the Act` `genuine grievance` `no other process available`

§§ `Money I am owed: monetary claims (ss 9, 10, 30-36)`
-- Situation: your employer, former employer or a superannuation trustee owes you money.

@export Can the South Australian Employment Court hear my claim for money from my employer, former employer or super fund (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) ss 9, 10, 33)?
GIVEN `what I claim` IS A `Claim kind` @desc What you are claiming: a sum due under the Act, an award, an enterprise agreement or your contract; a sum due under another workplace law (for example a public sector determination); compensation for unpaid superannuation contributions; a benefit from a superannuation trustee; a liquidated sum or debt under your common law contract of employment; damages for breach of that contract; or something else
      `that law gives a court jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a sum due under another workplace law: whether that law itself gives a court jurisdiction over the claim (for example long service leave under the Long Service Leave Act 1987)
      `conduct after 1 January 2026` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a sum due under another workplace law: whether the non-payment you claim for happened on or after 1 January 2026
      `same claim in another court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether proceedings on the same claim have begun in another court and have not been withdrawn or struck out
GIVETH A `Monetary claim answer`
`Can the Court hear my claim` MEANS `safw can the Court hear my claim (ss 9, 10, 33)` `what I claim` `that law gives a court jurisdiction` `conduct after 1 January 2026` `same claim in another court`

@export By what date must I make my monetary claim (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 31)?
GIVEN `payable on` IS A DATE @desc The day the sum you claim became payable (ISO 8601, e.g. 2025-03-15)
GIVETH A DATE
`By what date must I make my monetary claim` MEANS `safw the last day for a monetary claim (s 31)` `payable on`

@export Is my monetary claim in time (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 31)?
GIVEN `what I claim` IS A `Claim kind` @desc What you are claiming (see the choices); there is no time limit for unpaid superannuation contributions
      `payable on` IS A DATE @desc The day the sum you claim became payable
      `claimed on` IS A DATE @desc The day you made, or will make, the claim
GIVETH A `Limitation answer`
`Is my monetary claim in time` MEANS `safw is my monetary claim in time (s 31)` `what I claim` `payable on` `claimed on`

@export Can I make this monetary claim myself, or can someone make it for me or for a person who has died (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 32)?
GIVEN `who is claiming` IS A `Claimant position` @desc Who is making the claim: the person owed the money (an adult, or under 18); an association on their behalf; the personal representative of a person owed money who has died; a beneficiary of that person's estate; or someone else
GIVETH A `Who may claim answer`
`Who may make the monetary claim` MEANS `safw who may make the monetary claim (s 32)` `who is claiming`

@export If I win my monetary claim, will the judgment include interest (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 34)?
GIVEN `by consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judgment is given by consent of the parties
      `consent to interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether the parties consent to interest being awarded
      `I ask for interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you apply to the Court for interest
      `good reason not to` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds there is good reason not to include interest
GIVETH AN `Interest answer`
`Will my judgment include interest` MEANS `safw will my judgment include interest (s 34)` `by consent` `consent to interest` `I ask for interest` `good reason not to`

@export Over how many days must the interest on my judgment be worked out (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 34(1a))?
GIVEN `cause of action arose on` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action arose (usually when the sum fell due)
      `judgment on` IS A DATE @desc The day the judgment is delivered
GIVETH A NUMBER
`Over how many days must the interest be worked out` MEANS `safw the days interest must take into account (s 34(1a))` `cause of action arose on` `judgment on`

@export Can the money awarded to me be paid by instalments or into my super fund (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 35)?
GIVEN `what I claim` IS A `Claim kind` @desc What you are claiming (see the choices)
GIVETH AN `Instalments answer`
`How may the money be paid` MEANS `safw how may a monetary judgment be paid (s 35)` `what I claim`

@export Can I be ordered to pay the other side's legal costs of my monetary claim (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 36)?
GIVEN `what I claim` IS A `Claim kind` @desc What you are claiming (see the choices)
      `an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are an appeal
      `I acted vexatiously` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied you instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause
      `my unreasonable conduct caused costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that an unreasonable act or omission of yours caused the other party to incur the costs
GIVETH A `Costs answer`
`Can I be ordered to pay costs on my monetary claim` MEANS `safw can I be ordered to pay costs on my monetary claim (s 36)` `what I claim` `an appeal` `I acted vexatiously` `my unreasonable conduct caused costs`

@export Can I recover my own legal costs of my monetary claim (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 36)?
GIVEN `what I claim` IS A `Claim kind` @desc What you are claiming (see the choices)
      `an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are an appeal
      `they acted vexatiously` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the other party instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause
      `their unreasonable conduct caused my costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that an unreasonable act or omission of the other party caused you to incur the costs
GIVETH A `Costs answer`
`Can I recover my costs of my monetary claim` MEANS `safw can I recover my costs of my monetary claim (s 36)` `what I claim` `an appeal` `they acted vexatiously` `their unreasonable conduct caused my costs`

§§ `Redundancy: severance (s 72B)`
-- Situation: you have been told your job is going, or you have been made redundant.

@export Can I apply to SAET for an order applying the minimum standard for severance pay, and am I in time (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 72B(5), (6))?
GIVEN `who applies` IS A `Severance applicant` @desc Who makes the application: you (alone or with a group of employees), a registered association acting for you, or someone else
      `told of redundancy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given notice of a pending redundancy
      `told on` IS A DATE @desc If so: the day the notice was given
      `made redundant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employment has been terminated for redundancy
      `made redundant on` IS A DATE @desc If so: the day your employment was terminated
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied, or will apply, to SAET
GIVETH A `Severance answer`
`Can I apply for a severance order` MEANS `safw can I apply for a severance order (s 72B(5), (6))` `who applies` `told of redundancy` `told on` `made redundant` `made redundant on` `applied on`

§§ `Outworkers: unpaid remuneration from a responsible contractor (Part 3A Division 3)`
-- Situation: you are an outworker and have not been paid for work you did.

@export Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim against the business I believe gave out my work, and is it valid and in time (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) ss 99B, 99D)?
GIVEN `I am an outworker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an outworker under s 5 (see the outworker question)
      `outworkers brought in` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a provision of an award or enterprise agreement relates to outworkers, or a regulation extends the Act to outworkers
      `I believe they gave out the work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you believe on reasonable grounds that the business you are claiming against initiated an order for the work (other than as a retail purchaser) or distributed it, even through a chain of contracts
      `they only sell clothing by retail` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that business's sole business in the clothing industry is selling clothing (and associated items) by retail
      `work done on` IS A DATE @desc The (first) day you did the work you claim for
      `work finished on` IS A DATE @desc The day you completed the work
      `notice served on` IS A DATE @desc The day you served, or will serve, the written claim on the business
      `notice claims payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your written notice claims payment of the unpaid remuneration
      `notice has my name` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it sets out your name
      `notice has my address` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it sets out an address where you can be contacted
      `notice describes the work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it describes the work you performed
      `notice has the dates` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it sets out the date or dates you did the work
      `notice has the amount` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it sets out the amount of unpaid remuneration claimed for the work
      `statutory declaration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the particulars are verified by statutory declaration
GIVETH AN `Outworker claim answer`
`Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` MEANS
    `safw can I make an unpaid remuneration claim (ss 99B, 99D)` `I am an outworker` `outworkers brought in` `I believe they gave out the work` `they only sell clothing by retail` `work done on` `work finished on` `notice served on` `notice claims payment` `notice has my name` `notice has my address` `notice describes the work` `notice has the dates` `notice has the amount` `statutory declaration`

@export By what date must I serve my unpaid remuneration claim (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 99D(3))?
GIVEN `work finished on` IS A DATE @desc The day you completed the work you claim for (ISO 8601, e.g. 2025-03-15)
GIVETH A DATE
`By what date must I serve my unpaid remuneration claim` MEANS `safw the last day to serve an unpaid remuneration claim (s 99D(3))` `work finished on`

@export How much is the business I served my unpaid remuneration claim on still liable to pay me (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) ss 99E, 99F)?
GIVEN `amount claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of unpaid remuneration in your claim, in dollars
      `referred to my employer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business referred your claim to your employer (told you the employer's name and address in writing and served a copy on the employer)
      `days to refer` IS A NUMBER @desc If so: how many days after you served the claim it did so
      `employer paid me` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount the employer then paid you to accept liability, in dollars (0 if none)
      `days to pay` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days after being served with the referred claim the employer paid you
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much is the business still liable for` MEANS `safw the amount the apparent responsible contractor is liable for (ss 99E, 99F)` `amount claimed` `referred to my employer` `days to refer` `employer paid me` `days to pay`

@export If I take my unpaid remuneration claim to the South Australian Employment Court, must the Court order the business to pay (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 99G)?
GIVEN `still unpaid` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount the business is still liable for and has not paid, in dollars
      `they prove no work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business satisfies the Court that the work was not performed
      `they prove wrong amount` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business satisfies the Court that the amount claimed is not the correct amount for the work
GIVETH A `Recovery answer`
`Must the Court order the business to pay` MEANS `safw must the Court order the contractor to pay (s 99G)` `still unpaid` `they prove no work` `they prove wrong amount`

§§ `Civil penalty for unpaid entitlements (ss 104-104C)`
-- Situation: your employer has not paid wages, leave, super or other entitlements, and you want a penalty imposed.

@export Can I apply for a civil penalty order against my employer for not paying my entitlements, and is it in time (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) ss 104A, 104B)?
GIVEN `who applies` IS A `Civil penalty applicant` @desc Who applies: you, as an employee affected by the contravention; a registered association entitled to represent your industrial interests; an inspector; or someone else
      `contravention on` IS A DATE @desc The day the non-payment (the contravention) occurred
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day the application is, or will be, made
      `already penalised for this` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court has already made an order against the employer under a civil penalty provision of a State or Commonwealth Act for the same or substantially the same conduct
GIVETH A `Civil penalty application answer`
`Can I apply for a civil penalty order` MEANS `safw can a civil penalty application be made (s 104A)` `who applies` `contravention on` `applied on` `already penalised for this`

@export Can a civil penalty be imposed on my employer for not paying my entitlements (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 104(4), (5), s 104A(2))?
GIVEN `contravention proved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that the employer failed to pay an amount the Act, a workplace law or an industrial instrument requires it to pay for your work
      `deliberate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer's conduct was deliberate
      `Crown or company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer is the Crown or a body corporate
      `authorised the conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether it expressly, tacitly or impliedly authorised the conduct
      `systematic` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds the conduct was part of a systematic pattern of conduct relating to one or more employees
GIVETH A `Civil penalty answer`
`Can a civil penalty be imposed` MEANS `safw can a civil penalty be imposed (s 104(4), s 104A(2))` `contravention proved` (`safw the conduct was deliberate (s 104(4)(a), (5))` `deliberate` `Crown or company` `authorised the conduct`) `systematic`

@export What is the most civil penalty my employer can be ordered to pay for not paying my entitlements (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 104(1), s 104A(3)-(5))?
GIVEN `contravention proved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that the employer failed to pay an amount it was required to pay for your work
      `deliberate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer's conduct was deliberate
      `Crown or company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer is the Crown or a body corporate
      `authorised the conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether it expressly, tacitly or impliedly authorised the conduct
      `systematic` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds the conduct was part of a systematic pattern of conduct relating to one or more employees
      `how many contraventions` IS A NUMBER @desc How many contraventions (failures to pay) the application is about
      `how many after an earlier penalty` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of them were committed after the Court had already imposed a civil penalty on the employer for an earlier contravention of the same provision
      `one course of conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the contraventions arose out of one course of conduct by the employer
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most civil penalty` MEANS
    `safw the most the civil penalty can be (s 104(1), s 104A(3))`
        (`safw can a civil penalty be imposed (s 104(4), s 104A(2))` `contravention proved` (`safw the conduct was deliberate (s 104(4)(a), (5))` `deliberate` `Crown or company` `authorised the conduct`) `systematic`)
        (`safw contraventions counted (s 104A(4), (5))` `how many contraventions` `how many after an earlier penalty` `one course of conduct`)

@export Can I be ordered to pay costs in civil penalty proceedings about my entitlements (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 104C)?
GIVEN `I acted vexatiously` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied you instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause
      `my unreasonable conduct caused costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied an unreasonable act or omission of yours caused another party to incur the costs
GIVETH A `Civil penalty costs answer`
`Can I be ordered to pay costs in civil penalty proceedings` MEANS `safw costs in civil penalty proceedings (s 104C)` `I acted vexatiously` `my unreasonable conduct caused costs`

§§ `Unfair dismissal (Part 6, Schedule 8)`
-- Situation: your employer has dismissed you.

@export Can I claim unfair dismissal relief under the Fair Work Act 1994 (SA): does Part 6 apply to my dismissal (s 105A)?
GIVEN `the Act covers me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act applies to your employment (see the question on whether the Act applies)
      `apprentice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an apprentice or trainee under the South Australian Skills Act 2008
      `award or agreement covers me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employment is covered by an industrial instrument (an award or enterprise agreement under the Act, or a Commonwealth fair work instrument)
      `yearly pay` IS A NUMBER @desc Your remuneration a year immediately before the dismissal took effect, in dollars
      `threshold` IS A NUMBER @desc The s 105A(1)(a) threshold in force (the Act states $100 322 a year, indexed each 1 January under s 4(4); SAET publishes the current figure)
      `fixed term ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your contract was for a specified period or task and the employment ended at the end of that period or on completing the task
      `clear expectation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether the employer's conduct gave you a clear expectation of continuing employment
      `excluded by the regulations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulations exclude you from Part 6 (for example during a reasonable probation or qualifying period of up to 12 months, or as a short-term casual)
GIVETH A `Part 6 answer`
`Does Part 6 apply to my dismissal` MEANS `safw does Part 6 apply to my dismissal (s 105A)` `the Act covers me` `apprentice` `award or agreement covers me` `yearly pay` `threshold` `fixed term ended` `clear expectation` `excluded by the regulations`

@export By what date must I apply to SAET for unfair dismissal relief (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 106(1))?
GIVEN `dismissal took effect on` IS A DATE @desc The day your dismissal took effect (ISO 8601, e.g. 2025-03-15)
GIVETH A DATE
`By what date must I apply for unfair dismissal relief` MEANS `safw the last day to apply for unfair dismissal relief (s 106(1))` `dismissal took effect on`

@export Is my unfair dismissal application in time, and can I bring it (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 106)?
GIVEN `dismissal took effect on` IS A DATE @desc The day your dismissal took effect
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied, or will apply, to SAET
      `referred from elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceedings were referred to SAET by another adjudicating authority under another law (for example from equal opportunity proceedings)
      `other proceedings on foot` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have proceedings about the same dismissal on foot before another court, tribunal or authority that can grant relief for wrongful or unfair dismissal
GIVETH AN `Unfair dismissal time answer`
`Is my unfair dismissal application in time` MEANS `safw is my unfair dismissal application in time (s 106)` `dismissal took effect on` `applied on` `referred from elsewhere` `other proceedings on foot`

@export If I bring an unfair dismissal claim, what can SAET order (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) ss 108, 109)?
GIVEN `found unfair` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether SAET is satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that the dismissal was harsh, unjust or unreasonable
      `only redundancy pay inadequate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a redundancy payment was made under an industrial instrument and the only ground of complaint is that it was inadequate
      `former job not workable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether SAET finds re-employment in your former position impracticable, or not appropriate for some other reason
      `another job available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another position is available with the employer
      `no re-employment appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether SAET considers re-employment by the employer in any position would not be appropriate
      `cogent reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether SAET is satisfied there are cogent reasons to believe re-employment would not be appropriate in your case
      `under 50 employees` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether fewer than 50 employees are employed in the business or undertaking you would return to, at the time of SAET's decision
GIVETH A `Remedy answer`
`What can SAET order for my dismissal` MEANS `safw what can SAET order for my dismissal (ss 108, 109)` `found unfair` `only redundancy pay inadequate` `former job not workable` `another job available` `no re-employment appropriate` `cogent reasons` `under 50 employees`

@export What is the most compensation SAET can order for my unfair dismissal (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 109(3))?
GIVEN `yearly pay` IS A NUMBER @desc Your remuneration a year at the rate that applied immediately before the dismissal took effect, in dollars
      `indexed figure` IS A NUMBER @desc The s 109(3) figure in force (the Act states $33 100, indexed each 1 January under s 4(4); SAET publishes the current figure)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most compensation for my dismissal` MEANS `safw the compensation cap (s 109(3))` `yearly pay` `indexed figure`

@export If SAET orders that I be re-employed, how much back pay do I get for the time I was out (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 109(2)(a))?
GIVEN `weekly pay` IS A NUMBER @desc Your remuneration a week in the position from which you were dismissed, in dollars
      `dismissal took effect on` IS A DATE @desc The day your dismissal took effect
      `re-employed on` IS A DATE @desc The day you are re-employed
      `SAET directed otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether SAET has directed otherwise about pay for the period (then the Act does not fix the amount)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much back pay on re-employment` MEANS `safw back pay on re-employment (s 109(2)(a))` `weekly pay` `dismissal took effect on` `re-employed on` `SAET directed otherwise`

@export Can my unfair dismissal compensation be paid by instalments (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 109(4))?
GIVEN `exceptional circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether SAET is satisfied exceptional circumstances justify an instalment order
      `compensates lost pay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order compensates you for lost remuneration
      `as favourable as pay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether the instalments are at least as favourable to you as your pay would have been if the employment had continued
GIVETH AN `Instalment answer`
`Can my compensation be paid by instalments` MEANS `safw can my compensation be paid by instalments (s 109(4))` `exceptional circumstances` `compensates lost pay` `as favourable as pay`

@export Can I be ordered to pay costs in my unfair dismissal case (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 110)?
GIVEN `how it ended` IS A `How the case ended` @desc How the case ended: it went to a hearing; you discontinued it; or it is still running
      `unreasonable not to settle` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it went to a hearing: whether SAET is satisfied you clearly acted unreasonably in failing to discontinue or settle before the hearing concluded
      `days after conference` IS A NUMBER @desc If you discontinued: how many days after the conference of the parties concluded you did so
      `unreasonable to discontinue late` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you discontinued: whether SAET is satisfied you acted unreasonably
      `costs applied for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer (the other party) has applied for costs
      `days to apply for costs` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days after the determination or discontinuance the employer applied for costs
GIVETH AN `Unfair dismissal costs answer`
`Can I be ordered to pay costs in my unfair dismissal case` MEANS `safw can I be ordered to pay costs in my unfair dismissal case (s 110)` `how it ended` `unreasonable not to settle` `days after conference` `unreasonable to discontinue late` `costs applied for` `days to apply for costs`

@export By what date must SAET decide my unfair dismissal application (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 111)?
GIVEN `final submissions on` IS A DATE @desc The day the parties finished making their final submissions (the President may extend the time only for special reasons)
GIVETH A DATE
`By what date must SAET decide` MEANS `safw the last day for SAET's determination (s 111(1))` `final submissions on`

@export How many weeks' notice (or pay instead of notice) must my employer give me before terminating my employment (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) Schedule 8 cl 1)?
GIVEN `started on` IS A DATE @desc The day your continuous service with the employer began
      `terminated on` IS A DATE @desc The day your employment is terminated
      `born on` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `serious misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were guilty of serious misconduct, of a kind that makes it unreasonable to require the employer to keep you on during the notice period
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How many weeks' notice` MEANS `safw weeks of notice (Sch 8 cl 1)` `started on` `terminated on` `born on` `serious misconduct`

@export Did my employer follow the rule that I must be given a chance to respond before being dismissed for conduct or performance (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) Schedule 8 cl 2)?
GIVEN `conduct or performance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were dismissed for reasons related to your conduct or performance
      `given a chance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were given the opportunity to defend yourself against the allegations
      `could not be expected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer could not reasonably be expected to give you that opportunity
GIVETH A `Respond answer`
`Was I given the chance to respond` MEANS `safw was I given the chance to respond (Sch 8 cl 2)` `conduct or performance` `given a chance` `could not be expected`

§§ `Dismissal or prejudice for a prohibited reason, or for taking part in industrial proceedings (ss 115, 116B, 223, 233, 235)`
-- Situation: your employer acted against you because of union membership, a complaint, or industrial proceedings.

@export Did my employer commit an offence by acting against me for a prohibited reason, such as union membership or a complaint (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) ss 115, 116B)?
GIVEN `what they did` IS AN `Employer act` @desc What the employer did or threatened to do (see the choices)
      `why` IS A `Prohibited reason` @desc One of the reasons (it need only be one of several) for which the employer acted (see the s 115 choices)
      `absent for association duties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the reason was absence without leave: whether the absence was to carry out duties or exercise rights as an officer of an association
      `leave unreasonably refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether you applied for leave beforehand and it was unreasonably refused or withheld
GIVETH A `Prohibited reason answer`
`Was it done for a prohibited reason` MEANS `safw was it done for a prohibited reason (ss 115, 116B)` `what they did` `why` `absent for association duties` `leave unreasonably refused`

@export Did my employer commit an offence by dismissing or prejudicing me because I took part in SAET proceedings, an industrial dispute, or an award or agreement (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 223)?
GIVEN `what they did` IS A `Proceedings act` @desc What the employer did: dismissed you, threatened to, prejudiced you in your employment, threatened to, or something else
      `why` IS A `Proceedings reason` @desc The reason (see the choices)
      `because I broke a determination or committed an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer's ground was that you contravened a determination of SAET or committed an offence
GIVETH A `Proceedings answer`
`Was it done because I took part in industrial proceedings` MEANS `safw was it done because I took part in industrial proceedings (s 223)` `what they did` `why` `because I broke a determination or committed an offence`

@export Is it too late to prosecute my employer for an offence against the Act (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) s 235(2))?
GIVEN `offence on` IS A DATE @desc The day the offence is alleged to have been committed
      `prosecution started on` IS A DATE @desc The day the prosecution is, or will be, commenced
GIVETH A `Prosecution time answer`
`Is the prosecution in time` MEANS `safw is the prosecution in time (s 235(2))` `offence on` `prosecution started on`

@export My employer has been convicted of an offence against me under the Act: what can I apply to the court for (Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) ss 116B(2), 223(4), 233)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `Conviction offence` @desc The offence: s 116B (action for a prohibited reason), s 223 (discrimination for taking part in industrial proceedings), or another offence against the Act committed in respect of you
      `dismissed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were dismissed from your employment
GIVETH AN `After conviction answer`
`What can I apply for after the conviction` MEANS `safw what can I apply for after the conviction (ss 116B(2), 223(4), 233)` `the offence` `dismissed`
